1 Lexicals

   A lexical function returns an integer or a string: about the
   process, its files and devices, its logical names, the time, or a
   string it is given.  It is used in an expression (see Symbol_Assign
   Expressions) or between apostrophes ('F$TIME()').

   Format

     F$name([argument[,...]])

   The arguments are expressions, in parentheses and separated by
   commas.  An optional one may be left out, keeping its comma, and a
   function with no arguments still takes "()".  A name may be
   shortened while it names only one function (F$LEN is F$LENGTH; F$L
   is ambiguous).  A keyword argument (an item, a field) is a string,
   spelled in full, in either case.

2 F$CONFIG

   Returns an item of govax's configuration (see CONFIGURATION KEYS).
   F$CONFIG is govax's own, not VMS's.

   Format

     F$CONFIG(key [,operation])

   The key is a configuration item's name, such as "vax.library", in
   either case.  The operation is one of:

     VALUE      The item's value, or "" if it isn't set (the default).
     EXISTS     1 if it's set, else 0.
     BOOLEAN    1 if its value is TRUE, T, YES, Y, or 1 (any case),
                else 0.

   Example:

     $ IF F$CONFIG("vax.library", "EXISTS") THEN -
           WRITE SYS$OUTPUT F$CONFIG("vax.library")

2 F$CONTEXT

   Sets a criterion that F$PID's processes must meet.

   Format

     F$CONTEXT("PROCESS", context-symbol, item [,value] [,qualifier])

   The context symbol, "" for a new context, is then passed to F$PID,
   which returns only the processes whose item matches.  The items are
   $GETJPI's: PRCNAM, USERNAME, OWNER, GRP, PRI, UIC, CURPRIV, STATE,
   MODE, and others.  The qualifier says how the value matches: EQL or
   NEQ; for a number, also LSS, LEQ, GTR, or GEQ; for CURPRIV, ALL or
   ANY.  Each call adds a criterion, and the item CANCEL ends the
   context.  F$CONTEXT returns "".

   Example:

     $ CTX = ""
     $ X = F$CONTEXT("PROCESS", CTX, "PRCNAM", "SYSTEM*", "EQL")
     $ PID = F$PID(CTX)

2 F$CSID

   Not in govax: it reports cluster members, and govax has no cluster.
   It is DCL-W-LEXNOTIMPL.

2 F$CVSI

   Returns a bit field of a string as a signed integer.

   Format

     F$CVSI(start-bit, bits, string)

   Bits are counted from 0, the low bit of the string's first byte.

   Example:

     $ X = F$CVSI(0, 8, "A")          ! 65

2 F$CVTIME

   Converts a time to another format, or returns one of its fields.

   Format

     F$CVTIME([time] [,format] [,field])

   The time is an absolute, combination, or delta time; left out or "",
   it is now.  The format is one of:

     COMPARISON   yyyy-mm-dd hh:mm:ss.cc, which sorts as text (the
                  default).
     ABSOLUTE     dd-mmm-yyyy hh:mm:ss.cc.
     DELTA        dddd hh:mm:ss.cc; the time must be a delta time.

   The field is DATETIME (the default), DATE, TIME, YEAR, MONTH, DAY,
   HOUR, MINUTE, SECOND, HUNDREDTH, or, but for a delta time, WEEKDAY.

   Example:

     $ NOW = F$CVTIME(,,"TIME")
     $ TOMORROW = F$CVTIME("TOMORROW", "ABSOLUTE", "DATE")

2 F$CVUI

   Returns a bit field of a string as an unsigned integer.

   Format

     F$CVUI(start-bit, bits, string)

   Bits are counted from 0, the low bit of the string's first byte.

   Example:

     $ FLAGS = F$CVUI(4, 4, RECORD)

2 F$DEVICE

   Returns the next device that matches, then "" after the last.

   Format

     F$DEVICE([name] [,class] [,type] [,stream-id])

   The name may have the wildcards * and %; left out, every device
   matches.  The class is DISK, TERM, MAILBOX, or another of DEFINE
   /DEVICE's /DEVCLASS keywords, and the type a type such as RD54 (a
   type needs a class).  Each stream (0 without a stream-id) keeps its
   place between calls while the selection stays the same.

   Example:

     $ LOOP:
     $ DISK = F$DEVICE(, "DISK")
     $ IF DISK .EQS. "" THEN EXIT
     $ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT DISK
     $ GOTO LOOP

2 F$DIRECTORY

   Returns the default directory, such as [WORK].

   Format

     F$DIRECTORY()

2 F$EDIT

   Returns a string edited.

   Format

     F$EDIT(string, edit-list)

   The edits, separated by commas, are:

     COLLAPSE    removes every blank and tab
     COMPRESS    makes each run of blanks and tabs one blank
     LOWERCASE   makes letters lowercase
     TRIM        removes blanks and tabs at both ends
     UNCOMMENT   removes a comment, from a "!" on
     UPCASE      makes letters uppercase

   Text inside quotes is never edited, quotes and all.

   Example:

     $ LINE = F$EDIT(LINE, "TRIM,COMPRESS,UPCASE")

2 F$ELEMENT

   Returns one element of a string of elements separated by a
   delimiter.

   Format

     F$ELEMENT(number, delimiter, string)

   Elements are counted from 0, and the delimiter is one character.
   Past the last element, the result is the delimiter itself.

   Example:

     $ DAY = F$ELEMENT(2, ",", "MON,TUE,WED")     ! WED

2 F$ENVIRONMENT

   Returns a fact about the command environment.

   Format

     F$ENVIRONMENT(item)

   The items are:

     CAPTIVE            FALSE: govax has no captive accounts.
     CONTROL            The control characters SET CONTROL enables.
     DEFAULT            The default device and directory.
     DEPTH              The command level, 0 at the terminal.
     INTERACTIVE        TRUE.
     KEY_STATE          DEFAULT.
     MAX_DEPTH          The deepest command level allowed.
     MESSAGE            The parts of a message shown, as SET MESSAGE
                        writes them.
     NOCONTROL          The control characters SET NOCONTROL disables.
     ON_CONTROL_Y       TRUE if an ON CONTROL_Y is in effect.
     ON_SEVERITY        The severity ON acts at: WARNING, ERROR, SEVERE,
                        or NONE after SET NOON and at the terminal.
     PROCEDURE          The current command procedure's file, or "".
     PROMPT             The prompt.
     PROMPT_CONTROL     TRUE.
     PROTECTION         The default file protection.
     SYMBOL_SCOPE       LOCAL,GLOBAL.
     VERB_SCOPE         LOCAL,GLOBAL.
     VERIFY_IMAGE       TRUE if image verification is on.
     VERIFY_PREFIX      SET PREFIX's string.
     VERIFY_PROCEDURE   TRUE if procedure verification is on.

   Example:

     $ IF F$ENVIRONMENT("DEPTH") .EQ. 0 THEN EXIT

2 F$EXTRACT

   Returns part of a string.

   Format

     F$EXTRACT(start, length, string)

   The part starts at offset start, counting from 0, and is length
   characters long, fewer if the string ends first.  A start past the
   end, or a negative one, gives "", and a negative length the rest of
   the string.

   Example:

     $ YEAR = F$EXTRACT(0, 4, F$CVTIME())

2 F$FAO

   Returns a string formatted by $FAO's directives.

   Format

     F$FAO(control-string [,argument...])

   Each directive (!AS a string, !UL and !SL an integer in decimal, !XL
   in hexadecimal, !n< ... !> a field, !/ a new line, and the rest of
   $FAO's) takes the next argument, up to 15.  A string directive takes a
   string, and a numeric one an integer.

   Example:

     $ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT F$FAO("!AS has !UL block!%S", NAME, SIZE)

2 F$FILE_ATTRIBUTES

   Returns an attribute of a file on a mounted volume, from its header.

   Format

     F$FILE_ATTRIBUTES(filespec, item)

   Among the items: ORG (SEQ, REL, IDX), RFM (FIX, VAR, VFC, UDF, STM,
   STMLF, STMCR), RAT, MRS, LRL, FSZ, ALQ, DEQ, EOF, FFB, BKS, CDT, RDT,
   EDT, BDT, PRO, UIC, GRP, MBM, FID, DID, DVI, DIRECTORY, VERLIMIT,
   CTG, CBT, KNOWN, LOCKED, NOBACKUP, and NOK (the number of keys).

   Example:

     $ SIZE = F$FILE_ATTRIBUTES("REPORT.DAT", "EOF")

2 F$GETDVI

   Returns an item of a device's information, as $GETDVI does.

   Format

     F$GETDVI(device, item)

   The items are $GETDVI's, without DVI$_: DEVNAM, FULLDEVNAM, DEVCLASS,
   DEVTYPE, DEVCHAR, MNT, MOUNTCNT, VOLNAM, FREEBLOCKS, MAXBLOCK,
   CLUSTER, ERRCNT, OPCNT, REFCNT, OWNUIC, and others.  EXISTS is TRUE
   if there is such a device.

   Example:

     $ IF F$GETDVI("DUA1", "MNT") THEN -
           WRITE SYS$OUTPUT F$GETDVI("DUA1", "VOLNAM")

2 F$GETJPI

   Returns an item of a process's information, as $GETJPI does.

   Format

     F$GETJPI(pid, item)

   The pid is a process ID in hexadecimal, or "" for the current
   process.  The items are $GETJPI's, without JPI$_: PID, PRCNAM,
   USERNAME, UIC, OWNER, GRP, MASTER_PID, PRI, PRIB, STATE, MODE,
   JOBTYPE, TERMINAL, IMAGNAME, CURPRIV, AUTHPRIV, CPUTIM, BUFIO, DIRIO,
   PAGEFLTS, WSSIZE, WSQUOTA, WSEXTENT, PGFLQUOTA, PAGFILCNT, the
   address space's FREP0VA and FREP1VA (eight hexadecimal digits;
   FREP0VA is "" with no P0 pages), FREPTECNT, VIRTPEAK, and the quotas
   and limits.

   Example:

     $ ME = F$GETJPI("", "PRCNAM")

2 F$GETQUI

   Not in govax: it reports batch and print queues, and govax has none.
   It is DCL-W-LEXNOTIMPL.

2 F$GETSYI

   Returns an item of the system's information, as $GETSYI does.

   Format

     F$GETSYI(item [,node])

   The node, if given, must be this system's.  The items are $GETSYI's,
   without SYI$_: NODENAME, VERSION, BOOTTIME, SID, CPU, HW_NAME ("VAX
   (govax)"), CLUSTER_MEMBER, the paging parameters (MINWSCNT, GROWLIM,
   FREELIM, FREEGOAL, MPW_HILIMIT, PFCDEFAULT, ...), PAGEFILE_PAGE and
   PAGEFILE_FREE (the page file's size and free pages), and the global
   section limits GBLSECTIONS, GBLPAGES, and GBLPAGFIL with what's left,
   FREE_GBLSECTS, FREE_GBLPAGES, and CONTIG_GBLPAGES.

   Example:

     $ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "Running on ", F$GETSYI("NODENAME")

2 F$IDENTIFIER

   Converts a rights identifier's name to its value, or its value to
   its name.

   Format

     F$IDENTIFIER(identifier, conversion)

   The conversion is NAME_TO_NUMBER (an identifier the rights database
   doesn't have is 0) or NUMBER_TO_NAME (one it doesn't have is "").

2 F$INTEGER

   Returns an expression's value as an integer: a string that holds a
   number is that number, one starting with T or Y is 1, and any other
   is 0.

   Format

     F$INTEGER(expression)

2 F$LENGTH

   Returns a string's length.

   Format

     F$LENGTH(string)

2 F$LICENSE

   Not in govax: it reports software licenses, and govax has none.  It
   is DCL-W-LEXNOTIMPL.

2 F$LOCATE

   Returns the offset of a substring in a string, counting from 0, or
   the string's length if it isn't there.

   Format

     F$LOCATE(substring, string)

   Example:

     $ IF F$LOCATE(".", NAME) .EQ. F$LENGTH(NAME) THEN -
           NAME = NAME + ".DAT"

2 F$LOGICAL

   Returns a logical name's first equivalence string, from the process,
   job, group, and system tables, or "".  F$TRNLNM does more.

   Format

     F$LOGICAL(logical-name)

2 F$MESSAGE

   Returns a status's message text, as $GETMSG gives it, such as
   "%SYSTEM-S-NORMAL, normal successful completion".

   Format

     F$MESSAGE(status)

   Example:

     $ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT F$MESSAGE($STATUS)

2 F$MODE

   Returns the process's mode, INTERACTIVE.

   Format

     F$MODE()

2 F$PARSE

   Returns a file specification with the parts it leaves out filled in,
   or one part of it.

   Format

     F$PARSE(filespec [,default] [,related] [,field] [,type])

   The parts come from filespec, then the default specification, then
   the related specification's name and type, then the default device
   and directory.  The field is NODE, DEVICE, DIRECTORY, NAME, TYPE, or
   VERSION.  The type SYNTAX_ONLY doesn't check that the device and
   directory are there, and NO_CONCEAL shows the device a concealed
   logical name stands for, and any root before the directory
   (DUA1:[PROJECT.][SRC]A.DAT;).  The result is "" when the device isn't
   mounted, the directory isn't there (unless SYNTAX_ONLY), or the
   specification can't be parsed.

   Example:

     $ OUT = F$PARSE(".LIS", P1)           ! P1's name, type .LIS

2 F$PID

   Returns each process's PID in turn, then "" after the last.

   Format

     F$PID(context-symbol)

   The context symbol, set to "" before the first call, keeps the
   place; F$CONTEXT can make it choose only some processes.

   Example:

     $ CTX = ""
     $ LOOP:
     $ PID = F$PID(CTX)
     $ IF PID .EQS. "" THEN EXIT
     $ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT PID, " ", F$GETJPI(PID, "PRCNAM")
     $ GOTO LOOP

2 F$PRIVILEGE

   Returns TRUE if every privilege named is enabled, and every one
   named with NO isn't; FALSE otherwise.

   Format

     F$PRIVILEGE(privilege-list)

   Example:

     $ IF .NOT. F$PRIVILEGE("SYSPRV") THEN EXIT

2 F$PROCESS

   Returns the current process's name.

   Format

     F$PROCESS()

2 F$SEARCH

   Returns the next file that matches a file specification, then ""
   after the last.

   Format

     F$SEARCH(filespec [,stream-id])

   Each stream (0 without a stream-id) keeps its place between calls
   while the specification stays the same; a different one starts a
   new search, and after the "" the stream starts again.  A
   specification with no wildcards finds its file on every call.  A
   volume's files are written as DUA1:[WORK]LOGIN.COM;3, and host files
   as host paths.

   Example:

     $ IF F$SEARCH("TEMP.DAT") .NES. "" THEN DELETE TEMP.DAT;*

2 F$SETPRV

   Enables the privileges named, and disables those named with NO (ALL
   and NOALL are every privilege), and returns what they were, in the
   same form.  Privileges the process isn't authorized for aren't
   enabled.

   Format

     F$SETPRV(privilege-list)

   Example:

     $ SAVED = F$SETPRV("SYSPRV")
     $ ...
     $ X = F$SETPRV(SAVED)

2 F$STRING

   Returns an expression's value as a string; an integer is its decimal
   digits.

   Format

     F$STRING(expression)

2 F$TIME

   Returns the date and time now, in absolute format: dd-mmm-yyyy
   hh:mm:ss.cc.

   Format

     F$TIME()

2 F$TRNLNM

   Returns a logical name's translation, or an item of its information.

   Format

     F$TRNLNM(logical-name [,table] [,index] [,mode] [,case] [,item])

   The table is LNM$DCL_LOGICAL if not given: the process, job, group,
   and system tables.  The index chooses an equivalence string of a
   search list (0, the first, if not given).  The mode is USER (the
   default), SUPERVISOR, EXECUTIVE, or KERNEL.  The case is CASE_BLIND
   (the default) or CASE_SENSITIVE.  The item is VALUE (the default),
   ACCESS_MODE, CONCEALED, CONFINE, CRELOG, LENGTH, MAX_INDEX, NO_ALIAS,
   TABLE, TABLE_NAME, or TERMINAL.  A name that isn't there is "".

   Example:

     $ IF F$TRNLNM("NAMES") .EQS. "" THEN DEFINE NAMES EMPLOYEES.DAT

2 F$TYPE

   Returns INTEGER for a symbol whose value is an integer or a string
   that is one, STRING for another string, PROCESS_CONTEXT for a symbol
   F$CONTEXT made, and "" for a name that isn't a symbol.

   Format

     F$TYPE(symbol-name)

2 F$USER

   Returns the process's UIC, as an identifier where the rights database
   names it ([SYSTEM]), as numbers otherwise ([1,4]).

   Format

     F$USER()

2 F$VERIFY

   Returns whether procedure verification is on (1) or off (0), and
   then sets verification.

   Format

     F$VERIFY([procedure-value] [,image-value])

   An odd procedure-value turns procedure verification on, and an even
   one off; it sets image verification too unless image-value is given
   to set it.  Without procedure-value, image-value changes nothing.
   F$VERIFY in a comment line ('F$VERIFY(0)' after "$!") is still
   performed.

   Example:

     $ SAVED = F$VERIFY(0)
     $ ...
     $ X = F$VERIFY(SAVED)
